      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Darius and Alexander the Great</strong><br>\nThe most significant challenge to Darius III's reign came from Alexander the Great of Macedonia. In 334 BCE, Alexander crossed into Asia Minor, initiating a series of conflicts known as the Wars of Alexander the Great. Darius was determined to defend his empire against this invasion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Battle of Issus</strong><br>\nIn 333 BCE, Darius III faced Alexander the Great at the Battle of Issus. Despite having a larger army, Darius was defeated. This loss was devastating, and it allowed Alexander to gain control over much of Asia Minor. It also marked a turning point in the struggle between the Persian Empire and the Macedonian army.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Aftermath and Decline</strong><br>\nFollowing his defeat at Issus, Darius attempted to negotiate with Alexander, offering peace treaties and large ransoms for his family, who were captured during the battle. Alexander rejected these offers, demanding unconditional surrender from Darius, who refused to give in.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
