          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Darius's Flight and Downfall</strong><br>\nThe empire continued to crumble as Alexander advanced. Darius was forced to flee eastward, trying to rally support for another stand against the Macedonians. As he moved further east, he lost more of his empire and his support weakened.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Battle of Gaugamela</strong><br>\nDarius's final stand against Alexander occurred at the Battle of Gaugamela in 331 BCE. Once again outnumbered and outmaneuvered, Darius's army was crushed by Alexander's forces. Following this decisive defeat, Darius's rule was effectively over, and he became a fugitive within his own land.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death of Darius III</strong><br>\nDarius III was eventually betrayed and killed by one of his satraps, Bessus, in 330 BCE. His death marked the end of the Achaemenid dynasty, and he was remembered in history as the last emperor of the once-great Persian Empire. Alexander, who continued his campaign, declared himself successor to the Achaemenid rulers, taking over the vast empire Darius once led.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
